From 4fc0a60e6e0b834d2fccccb4402ff054c5525d95 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: z060142 <z060142@yahoo.com.tw>
Date: Sat, 29 Aug 2026 03:43:46 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 111/119] fix: area lights fell off as the fourth power of
 distance, and the rectangle was 2pi over its own disc

CE multiplies the LTC form factor - which already contains the inverse square, E ~ A
cos cos / d^2 - by GetPhysicalLightAttenuation's (r/d)^2 and by the CPU's matching
x121. Two inverse squares: an area light is 1.0 stop under a point light of the same
value at 2 m and 5.6 stops under it at 10 m, and the only way to make one look right
is to enlarge it until its area happens to cancel the distance. That is the user's
own G3 diagnosis, and it is correct.

Three separate defects, all on the ReC path only, all byte-identical off it:

1. The double falloff. TiledShading.cfi's deferred and forward area branches take a
   new GetAreaLightFadeout() - the radius fadeout ONLY - instead of the full physical
   attenuation. The fadeout is kept and must be: it is what makes the light reach
   exactly zero at its clip radius instead of popping at the tile boundary.

2. The per-shape LTC normalisation. LTC_Rectangle ends at len*scale, and by Lambert's
   polygon formula len = 2E - twice the cosine-weighted solid angle; LTC_Disk ends at
   formFactor*scale, which is E/PI exactly. S2's single SCENE_BRDF_NORM_FACTOR
   therefore left the rectangle 1 stop hot and the disc 1.65 stops dark, and the two
   shapes 2.65 stops apart from each other. AreaLightLTC_GGX now applies 1/(2PI) to
   the rectangle and exactly 1.0 to the disc, so both deliver E/PI - the same quantity
   DiffuseBRDF delivers. LTCTex_2.w is the horizon clip and hides neither constant.

3. The value's semantics. TiledLightVolumes.cpp gives DLF_AREA (1+bulb)^2 / A instead
   of the point light's intensityMul, so an area light at Diffuse Multiplier v now
   delivers exactly the illuminance a POINT light at v delivers, at the same distance,
   at any panel size and either shape. That is Frostbite's L = PHI/(A*PI) and UE's and
   HDRP's default rect mode: resize the softbox and the brightness holds, only the
   shadows soften. Luminance (nits) semantics is the same code with the division
   removed, and is what a per-light unit enum would select later.

The cull radius follows: the radiation radius itself is already right, because the /A
here and the *A in the form factor cancel, but every distance in the system is measured
from the light's ORIGIN while an area light emits from a surface - so posRad.w gains
the emitting half-diagonal, on the render thread where the switch is visible rather
than in CryCommon's ComputeEffectiveRadius(), which also drives shadow allocation.

Two stock area-light bugs fixed under the same gate: the specular cosine used an
UNNORMALISED centre vector (saturate() killed it beyond a metre), and that centre was
built from raw world components, so a rotated rectangle got the wrong one.

New shader macro SCENE_REFERRED_LIGHT_UNITS - CV_SceneExposure.z < 0, exact on both
paths - because the per-shape constant is not a scaling of 1/PI.

docs/SceneReferredContent.md section 7 declares what every light type's authored number
means today and on the ReC path, with a fixture table (a 100 W bulb is 0.0105, a 2 kW
HMI open face is 1.45) and the known inconsistencies left standing: volumetric fog and
ambient area lights use the legacy nearest-point model, particles are not lit by area
lights at all, and e_LightIlluminanceThreshold still cuts at 40 lux.

Notes: scene-notes/decisions/s8-light-units.md, research/s8-light-units.md.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus <noreply@anthropic.com>
