+
+## 7. What a light's number actually means (Stage S8, D4)
+
+Only two things in CRYENGINE 5.7 ship with a real photometric unit: the sun's
+`PARAM_SUN_INTENSITY`, which is **lux**, and a material's `Emittance`, which is **kcd/m²**
+(1.0 = 1 000 nits). Nothing else — no light component property carries a unit, a tooltip or a
+range that means anything. This section writes down what the numbers do mean, and what the
+scene-referred path changes about them.
+
+The one engine-wide constant behind all of it: **1.0 engine unit = 10 000 lux, or 10 000 cd/m²,
+depending on which side of the BRDF you are standing** (`RENDERER_LIGHT_UNIT_SCALE`).
+
+### 7.1 The table
+
+| Light type | The authored number | What it means on the **stock** path | What it means on the **ReC** path |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| **Sun** | `PARAM_SUN_INTENSITY` (time of day) | **Illuminance in lux.** Correct, and the anchor everything else is calibrated against. Default 119 000 lux ≈ clear midday | unchanged (the ÷π moves into the BRDF; the number is the same lux) |
+| **Point light** | `Diffuse Multiplier` × `Color` | **Luminous intensity**, in units of `10⁴/(1+r)²` cd where `r` is the Attenuation Bulb Size. At the default 0.1 m bulb, **1.0 = 12 100 cd**. Equivalently: 10 000 lux measured 1 m *from the bulb surface* | unchanged. What changes is only that the 1/π now sits in the BRDF, so every local light delivers 1.65 stops less than it used to — one convention, applied to all of them |
+| **Projector / spot** | same | the same **on-axis intensity**, and it is **independent of the cone angle**. Narrowing the beam does not concentrate the energy — the cone only masks. (This is Frostbite's deliberate choice, not a CE accident; UE does divide by the cone solid angle, and that is the difference to expect when converting content) | unchanged |
+| **Area light — rectangle** | same | **No unit.** The LTC form factor behaves as a surface *luminance* (double the panel, double the light), and the engine then multiplies it by a point light's `(1+r)²/d²` as well — so the value is `nits · lux`, which is not a photometric quantity, and the light falls off as **d⁻⁴**. It is also `2π` (2.65 stops) hotter than its own disc for the same value | **The same luminous intensity as a point light.** A rectangle at `Diffuse Multiplier = v` delivers the same illuminance, at the same distance, as a point light at `v`. Falloff is inverse-square. Resizing the panel keeps its brightness and only softens its shadows |
+| **Area light — disc / ellipse** | same | as the rectangle, minus 2.65 stops (the missing `2π`), and its emitting radius is `Width/4`, not `Width/2` — so a `Width = Height = 1` disc is **7.6 stops** under a 1 × 1 rectangle | identical to the rectangle: same value, same distance, same reading. The `Width/4` radius is unchanged, because the editor gizmo draws the same figure; dividing by the area the shader actually integrates is what makes the two shapes agree |
+| **Ambient light** | `Diffuse Multiplier` × `Color` | **Dimensionless**, and correctly so — it is a multiplicative tint over the accumulator, with its own decision boundary at 1.0. Ambient *area* lights use a different, legacy shading model | unchanged, and deliberately excluded from every part of the scene-referred light work |
+| **Environment probe** | `Diffuse`/`Specular Multiplier` | **Dimensionless gain** over a baked absolute radiance. The cube itself carries engine units inherited from the scene it was baked in | unchanged. See section 4 for the bake convention |
+| **Emissive material** | `Emittance` colour × strength | **Luminance in kcd/m²**: 1.0 = 1 000 cd/m². Correct | unchanged |
+| **Fog volume emission** | emission colour | **kcd/m²**, same as emissive. Correct | unchanged |
+
+### 7.2 Reading the table for real fixtures
+
+Because a point light's `1.0` is 12 100 cd — roughly 150 000 lumens, a stadium lamp — every real
+practical lives near the bottom of the slider. At the default 0.1 m bulb:
+
+| Fixture | Photometry | `Diffuse Multiplier` |
+|---|---|---|
+| 100 W tungsten bulb | 1 600 lm → 127 cd | **0.0105** |
+| 1 000 lm LED panel | Φ/π = 318 cd on axis | **0.0263** |
+| 2 kW HMI, open face | 220 000 lm → 17 507 cd | **1.45** |
+| 2 kW HMI in a 30° Fresnel | ≈ 1.03 Mcd | **85** |
+| *(reference)* sun | 119 000 lux | `PARAM_SUN_INTENSITY 119000` |
+
+On the ReC path the same numbers are valid for an area light of any size and either shape — which
+is the whole point of section 7.1's last two rows.
+
+### 7.3 What this changes about existing content
+
+Under `Scene Referred`, area lights that already exist will look different, and by a lot:
+
+* a 1 × 1 m rectangle is **1 stop darker at 1 m, 2.2 stops brighter at 3 m, 5.6 stops brighter at
+  10 m** than it was — the d⁻⁴ is gone;
+* a rectangle is **1 stop darker** and a disc **1.65 stops brighter** relative to a point light,
+  because each shape now carries its own correct LTC normalisation instead of one shared `1/π`;
+* a panel that is not 1 × 1 changes by the ratio of its area to 1 m²: a 2 × 2 loses 2 stops, a
+  0.5 × 0.5 gains 2 — that is the size-invariance being switched on, and it is what makes the
+  number mean an intensity.
+
+This is a deliberate, documented, path-dependent change, exactly like the 1.65-stop shift on point
+lights. The stock path is untouched, byte for byte.
+
+Two long-standing engine bugs in the area-light specular are also fixed, on the ReC path only: the
+cosine term used an *unnormalised* vector (so it saturated to 1.0 for any light more than about a
+metre away), and the light centre was taken from raw world components, so a *rotated* rectangle got
+the wrong centre.
+
+### 7.4 Known inconsistencies, recorded rather than hidden
+
+* **Volumetric fog** lights area sources with the engine's *legacy* nearest-point-on-rectangle
+  model, not LTC. An area light's contribution to fog therefore does not follow the fix above.
+* **Ambient area lights** use that same legacy model and are excluded by design.
+* **Particles** are not lit by area or ambient light entities at all — they never enter the
+  light-volume list.
+* `e_LightIlluminanceThreshold` still cuts a light off at **40 lux**, which is generous for a
+  daylight scene and far too aggressive for a night one. It is a cvar; lower it for night work.
